# These are consumed on Linux. CRLF would break shell scripts outright
# ("bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M").
#
# The catch-all comes first so files with no extension are covered too:
# rootfs/usr/local/bin/w01-wifi and scripts/fel-emmc.py have no .sh suffix,
# and without this a clone on Windows (core.autocrlf=true, the default there)
# checks them out with CRLF and they will not run.
*         text=auto eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
LICENSE   text eol=lf

# Patches must be stored byte for byte. 0003 patches driver sources that are
# CRLF upstream, so 704 of its context lines end in CR. Normalising those to
# LF makes the context stop matching and "git apply" fails with "patch does
# not apply" for everyone who clones, while still looking fine on the machine
# that generated it.
*.patch   -text

*.bin     binary
*.png     binary
*.tar.gz  binary
